## Seven new findings — original Phase-1 contribution

# WEC

*DE67 1 owner intent brief — camp observation, dispatch behavior, NPC reactions, and basecamp performance*

## Desired playtest outcome

The player can start from the camp scenario, make ordinary choices, and see a living world. Staffed camps and active dispatches observe signals independently while loaded or abstract. Valid first smoke and first light can both initiate the cannibal response. Dispatches keep their members and travel progress when the reality bubble moves; they search, fight, and genuinely return instead of freezing or vanishing. Patrol settings reflect patrol duty and restore afterward. A stalker under attack reacts. Waiting or sleeping at a camp with two assigned NPCs remains responsive while those NPCs keep doing their work and react to danger.

## Scenario starts and evidence boundaries

- **Owner's untouched reference starts on the Mac mini:** [open-window world](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-open-windows-20260924-122356/save/TestSetup00/) and [its README](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-open-windows-20260924-122356/README.txt); [closed-window world](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-closed-windows-20260924-122642/save/TestSetup00/) and [its README](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-closed-windows-20260924-122642/README.txt). The closed version is the preferred smoke start; its README records owner-reported complete darkness. Window state is owner-confirmed, not independently replayed. Copy either archive before playtesting.
- **Camp-modified open-window scenario on Windows:** [TestSetup00 world](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/save/TestSetup00/) and [setup audit](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/audit/camp-scenario-setup.json). The audit records a bandit camp transplanted to `(127,137,0)` and a cannibal camp to `(135,137,0)` near the player at `(131,143,0)`. The cannibal special reused a bandit-camp source footprint but was registered as `cannibal_camp`. These nearby hostile camps were **tacked onto a copy** of the open-window reference for this scenario; they are not part of the untouched Mac reference start. The Mac open-window overmap matches the [Windows pre-transplant backup](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/audit/o.0.0-original.zzip), while the modified Windows overmap differs. The Windows README describes window state but does not document the added camps. Use this derived start for the observed camp and dispatch behaviors.
- **Observed playtest checkpoint:** [current TestSetup00 world](.userdata/windows-camp-fixed-20260924/save/TestSetup00/) and [debug log](.userdata/windows-camp-fixed-20260924/config/debug.log). This is a later, mutable save containing the observed dispatch and encounters. It is evidence for what happened, not either untouched reference start.

The playtests below are **targets for DE67 2/3**, not tests already run or permission to implement. Each should bind the copied start, build, elapsed game time, relevant actor IDs, and loaded/abstract state. Use ordinary gameplay for the behavior being claimed; keep any controlled setup separate from the result.

## 1. Bandit dispatch gets stuck returning

**Observed problem.** The bandit structural dispatch from `(127,137,0)` has two surviving members, IDs 4 and 5, in `returning_home`. It remains at waypoint 3 of a five-OMT route, with current route tile `(126,135,0)` about two OMTs from camp. Repeated [log entries](.userdata/windows-camp-fixed-20260924/config/debug.log#L586254) show zero homeward steps or crossings and no paired loaded entry or staging positions. `missing_deadline_elapsed` means its deadline has already passed.

**Observed working.** The outing was created, both member identities survive, and its homeward route and state are retained. The problem is that travel stops near camp.

**Code clue.** [Loaded entry search](src/do_turn.cpp#L6378) requires two adjacent valid squares on the exact route OMT, which is outside the current loaded map. The [route-edge fallback](src/do_turn.cpp#L6492) is unavailable for this outing (`abstract_resume=no`, `cohesion_assembled=no`, `recenter_gate=no`), and [materialization is skipped](src/do_turn.cpp#L6695). This explains the repeated failure; it does not make abstract travel a completed return.

**Change wanted.** Keep the same surviving pair moving home abstractly while its route OMT is outside the loaded bubble. Materialize the same people at a valid loaded entry when appropriate, and resume abstract travel if the bubble moves away. Mark the return complete only after genuine arrival at camp.

**Targeted playtest.** Start from a copy of the [camp-modified open-window scenario](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/save/TestSetup00/) for a natural dispatch and return; also use a copy of the [observed checkpoint](.userdata/windows-camp-fixed-20260924/save/TestSetup00/) to exercise the already-stuck pair. Move the bubble across the home route and wait through return. Check continuing waypoints, pair IDs, loaded/abstract handoff, casualties, and actual camp arrival. A placement failure must leave a truthful active journey, not a frozen cursor or false success.

## 2. First smoke signal fails to initiate the cannibal response

**Reported problem.** Josef reports that smoke arriving as the first signal did not initiate the cannibal camp at `(135,137,0)`. The previously rejected smoke packet alone did not prove that a valid camp-reaching smoke signal was ignored.

**Observed working.** First light did initiate the camp: three NPCs materialized and a two-person scout dispatch formed. Rain and that later successful light response are not reasons to dismiss the smoke-first case.

**Code clue.** [Signal sampling](src/live_light.cpp#L45) discovers smoke and light, then passes only `light_samples` to the cannibal delivery callback. The downstream contact dispatcher can accept smoke, but a smoke-only first packet does not reach it through this path ([callback owner](src/do_turn.cpp#L9065)).

**Change wanted.** A valid first smoke signal reaches the same camp response decision as first light. It can initiate needed NPC materialization and dispatch without prior light, under the same ownership and duplicate-prevention rules.

**Targeted playtest.** Start from a disposable copy of the [Mac closed-window reference world](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-closed-windows-20260924-122642/save/TestSetup00/). Add the same nearby cannibal camp to that copy as controlled setup, recording its placement; the untouched reference is not itself the camp-modified scenario. Make smoke the first valid signal and verify that it actually reaches the camp before judging the response; check camp activation, concrete NPCs if needed, dispatch creation, and no duplicate response. Run the first-light comparison from a separate copied start. An out-of-range or rejected smoke sample is inconclusive.

## 3. A living camp is omitted as an observer

**Observed problem.** The ecology observer displayed the cannibal camp as active and populated, yet [staffed-camp observation](.userdata/windows-camp-fixed-20260924/config/debug.log#L99) repeatedly logged `sites=3 eligible=0 callbacks=0`. The counter stayed zero after a scout dispatch appeared.

**Observed working.** The saved camp had a valid living roster: five abstract members before materialization and, after the scout pair left, one healthy member at home. The display recognized that living camp. Abstract/unloaded status alone does not mean unstaffed, and dispatch creation showed that this camp could act.

**Code clue.** [Camp observer selection](src/bandit_live_world.cpp#L15505) requires a healthy concrete member marked `at_home`, so the abstract staffed camp is missed. It then skips the camp when an outing is active, despite a remaining home member. The [ecology display](src/ecology_debug_view.cpp#L494) uses the valid roster and living count, explaining the mismatch.

**Change wanted.** **Staffed camps and active dispatches are the complete observer set.** A valid living home roster makes a camp an observer whether abstract or loaded and whether or not a dispatch exists. An active dispatch observes separately. Show the real exclusion reason if either is ineligible.

**Targeted playtest.** From the [camp-modified open-window start](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/save/TestSetup00/), check camp observation before any dispatch, while members are abstract, after materialization, and while a dispatch is away. Repeat across a reality-bubble transition. A staffed camp and its active dispatch should each remain eligible without creating extra observers.

## 4. Patrol NPCs must investigate sounds only during patrol

**Observed status.** This is an owner-requested behavior, not a demonstrated setting failure in the current playtest. No before/after patrol setting trace has been captured.

**Current code.** [Patrol order transitions](src/npc.cpp#L4696) change mission and order state without changing the follower sound rule. [Sound handling](src/npctalk.cpp#L2105) bypasses `ignore_noise` for a roaming patrol guard, so behavior can disagree with the visible setting and fixed posts do not receive the same exception.

**Change wanted.** On active patrol, both the visible **Investigate sounds** setting and behavior are ON. On shift end, reassignment, interruption, or disabling patrol, restore that NPC's own previous ON/OFF value. Save/load preserves the temporary state and prior value. Reserve workers keep their settings.

**Targeted playtest.** Use a copy of the [Mac open-window owner reference](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-open-windows-20260924-122356/save/TestSetup00/) if it has suitable player-camp workers; otherwise prepare a separate disposable camp setup. Exercise an NPC whose setting began OFF and one that began ON, both roaming and fixed posts, an audible event, patrol exit, and save/reload during patrol. Verify sound response and displayed setting at each transition.

## 5. Hostile dispatches should search, fight, and leave

**Observed problem.** Josef saw a cannibal standing near the middle of a target tile instead of visibly searching it. The saved scout pair was already in `returning_home` with local ownership, so that one stationary moment may be a failure to depart rather than a failure to scout. Its exact immediate cause is unverified.

**Observed working.** In this playtest one hostile NPC attacked while another fled. That different individual survival choice is welcome behavior, not a bug. A finite scout return time already exists.

**Code clue.** The [loaded local gate](src/do_turn.cpp#L4813) handles exposure and player contact, while the [loaded raid target list](src/do_turn.cpp#L2616) focuses on the player and allies. These paths do not establish the requested full search or cannibal human-and-zombie target rule.

**Change wanted.** A visiting cannibal or bandit dispatch moves through and scans the area, makes contact, then leaves after a bounded visit. Cannibals aggressively seek detected non-cannibal humans and zombies and fight animals hostile to them; they ignore harmless birds and wildlife. Bandits use their own enemy relationships and defend against hostile animals. Preserve each member's fear, combat choice, identity, and casualties. Search and departure progress continue abstractly when unloaded; a failed local move neither freezes nor falsely finishes the visit.

**Targeted playtest.** Start a natural hostile dispatch from a copy of the [camp-modified open-window world](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/save/TestSetup00/). Observe arrival, movement through the tile, contact, withdrawal, and return while moving the reality bubble. Use separate controlled disposable encounters for non-cannibal humans, zombies, a hostile animal, and harmless wildlife; do not infer one target rule from the player-only fight. Confirm that an NPC may flee while the group's visit still resolves.

## 6. Writhing stalker absorbs repeated spear attacks

**Observed problem.** In daylight, Josef hit a writhing stalker four times with a two-square spear before it died. It did not visibly close on Josef, evade, or withdraw from the repeated hits.

**Observed working and limit.** During the first hit it tried to claw Cyril Crowder, so it was acting on another target rather than wholly inert. The exact decision gate it selected on subsequent turns was not logged.

**Code clue.** The [local planner](src/monmove.cpp#L1287) receives one generic-plan target; some decisions [clear destination](src/monmove.cpp#L1419). [Recent counterpressure](src/writhing_stalker_ai.cpp#L192) adds caution but does not itself require movement or retargeting. This is a plausible explanation, not a proven per-turn trace.

**Change wanted.** When a nearby human attacks it, promptly reconsider that attacker and its exposure. Daylight caution can favor striking, cover, or withdrawal, but repeated reachable attacks should not produce an unexplained stationary hold.

**Targeted playtest.** The [camp-modified open-window world](.userdata/windows-camp-20260924/save/TestSetup00/) is the observed campaign origin, but the saved stalker encounter was not captured before contact. Build a disposable daylight scene with another human drawing stalker attention and the player at spear reach two. Attack through several advancing turns and observe actual target, movement, attack/evade/withdrawal, and consequences. Do not count a scripted decision or a load-only check as the playtest.

## 7. Waiting or sleeping at basecamp slows with two assigned NPCs

**Observed problem.** Josef reports that waiting or sleeping inside his camp becomes noticeably slow with as few as two NPCs assigned. No timing comparison or profiler trace has yet isolated the cost.

**Observed working and limit.** The NPCs can be assigned to camp, but this observation does not establish which routine work is expensive. The desired improvement must keep their jobs, interaction, patrol, danger response, and loaded/abstract continuity intact.

**Code clues, not a measured cause.** [Loaded NPC turns](src/do_turn.cpp#L11290) process each active NPC and can make multiple movement decisions. An assigned worker can select downtime and still enter the [nearby item search](src/npcmove.cpp#L2784), which [scans tiles](src/npcmove.cpp#L5265) when pickup is allowed. If the camp has a locker zone, [downtime checks](src/basecamp.cpp#L4154) build a full live gear state [before the service-due check](src/basecamp.cpp#L4223). These are possible repeated-work targets, not proof of this save's bottleneck.

**Change wanted.** Waiting and sleeping with two assigned camp NPCs advance at a comfortable pace, and the result continues to hold as the camp grows. Remove redundant work while preserving real NPC behavior and reactions; do not solve the slowdown by making the camp inert.

**Targeted playtest.** Use matching disposable copies of the [Mac open-window owner reference](/Volumes/CodexBulk/Schanigarten/workspaces/Cataclysm-AOL/.userdata/reference-saves/josef-basecamp-open-windows-20260924-122356/save/TestSetup00/) with the same window state: before and after assigning two NPCs, advance the same game-time span by waiting and sleeping. Record elapsed wall time per game-time span and visible NPC work, sound/danger reactions, and interaction availability. Profile the expensive path before choosing an optimization; no fixed acceptable slowdown has been set by the owner.

## Handoff

This brief records owner outcomes and the observed starting points. DE67 2 should resolve implementation ownership and the unverified causes, then turn each targeted playtest into an honest verification route. A reported symptom, code clue, or prepared save is not behavioral proof. This DE67 1 brief authorizes no code implementation and does not start DE67 3.
